Write the rational expression as $(x^2+4)^{-1}$ and apply the <chain rule>:
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}\frac1{x^2+4}=-\frac{2x}{(x^2+4)^2}.}
$$
The denominator is positive for every real $x$, so the <derivative> is defined throughout the real line.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The outer <exponential function> differentiates to itself, and the inner <sine> has <derivative> <cosine>. The <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}e^{\sin x}=e^{\sin x}\cos x.}
$$

