= Solution
{parent=a}

For positive $a$, write $a^{-x}=\exp(-x\ln a)$. Differentiating by the <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}a^{-x}=-(\ln a)a^{-x}.}
$$
The <natural logarithm> of the constant base supplies the factor; it must not be omitted when $a\ne e$.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Set $u=\ln x$, so $du=dx/x$. The real domain is $x>1$, ensuring $u>0$. The <integration by substitution> followed by <integration by parts> gives
$$
\int\frac{\ln(\ln x)}x\,dx=\int\ln u\,du=u\ln u-u+C.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\ln x\,\ln(\ln x)-\ln x+C,\qquad x>1.}
$$

