= Solution
{parent=a}

Since the <derivative> of $\ln\cos x$ is $-\tan x$ on this interval,
$$
\int_0^{\pi/4}\tan x\,dx
=\big[-\ln\cos x\big]_0^{\pi/4}
=-\ln\frac1{\sqrt2}=\boxed{\frac12\ln2}.
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the real <antiderivative> $\ln|x|$, valid on either half-line away from zero:
$$
\int_{-2}^{-1}\frac{dx}{x}
=\big[\ln|x|\big]_{-2}^{-1}=\ln1-\ln2=\boxed{-\ln2}.
$$
The negative answer is consistent with a negative integrand and increasing <integration> limits.

