= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The specified range ensures $\cos y\ne0$, so the <separable differential equation> can be written $\sec^2y\,dy=\sin x\,dx$. Integrating gives $\tan y=C-\cos x$. The range selects the principal <inverse tangent>, hence
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\arctan(C-\cos x),\qquad C\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Every finite $C$ produces a solution within the required range. Constant solutions at the excluded values $y=\pm\pi/2$ are not part of this family or of the question's domain.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <general solution> of this first-order <linear ordinary differential equation> is $y=Ce^{3x}$. Its <initial condition> gives $C=3$, so
$$
\boxed{y(x)=3e^{3x}.}
$$
<Differentiation> verifies $y'=3y$ and substitution at zero gives the required value.

