= Solution
{parent=a}

The <sphere> has surface area $4\pi R^2=400\pi\,\mathrm{m^2}$. Dividing the given surface-patch area by that total gives the dimensionless fraction
$$
\boxed{\frac{10}{400\pi}=\frac1{40\pi}\approx0.00796,}
$$
or about **$0.796\%$** of the surface. The patch area is already measured on the surface, so no planar projection or spherical-cap conversion is needed.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Interpret the stated side lengths as all eight edges of the <square pyramid>. The equal lateral edges put its apex above the base <centre>. The base-centre-to-vertex distance is half the diagonal, $1/\sqrt2\,\mathrm m$. The <Pythagorean theorem> therefore gives
$$
h=\sqrt{1^2-(1/\sqrt2)^2}=\frac1{\sqrt2}\,\mathrm m.
$$
Using the <pyramid> volume formula with a $1\,\mathrm{m^2}$ base,
$$
\boxed{V=\frac13(1)\frac1{\sqrt2}
=\frac1{3\sqrt2}\,\mathrm{m^3}\approx0.2357\,\mathrm{m^3}.}
$$

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
