= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Where the original expression is defined, multiplication by $\cos x$ gives
$$
y=\frac{\cos x}{\cos x+\sin x}
=\frac12\left[1+\cot\left(x+\frac\pi4\right)\right].
$$
Thus the graph repeats with period $\pi$ and has vertical <asymptotes>
$$
\boxed{x=-\frac\pi4+k\pi,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Between consecutive <asymptotes> the extended <cotangent> branch decreases from $+\infty$ to $-\infty$, since $y'=-1/(\cos x+\sin x)^2<0$. It passes through $(k\pi,1)$ and $(\pi/4+k\pi,1/2)$.

It is essential to <preserve the domain when simplifying a function>. At $x=\pi/2+k\pi$, the original tangent is undefined. The finite limiting value is zero, so the graph has **holes at $(\pi/2+k\pi,0)$**, rather than actual zeros. The simplified expression extends across those points, but the original one does not.

\Image[paper-1-tangent-quotient.png]
{title=The periodic tangent quotient, with vertical asymptotes and the original domain's holes retained}

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

This <exponential function> is positive everywhere, with $y(0)=1$, $y\to\infty$ as $x\to-\infty$ and $y\to0$ as $x\to\infty$. Its <derivatives> are
$$
y'=-3x^2e^{-x^3}\leq0,\qquad
y''=3x(3x^3-2)e^{-x^3}.
$$
It is strictly decreasing overall, with a horizontal stationary <inflection point> at **$(0,1)$**. A second, nonstationary <inflection point> occurs at $x=(2/3)^{1/3}$, where $y=e^{-2/3}$. The only horizontal <asymptote> is **$y=0$ toward positive infinity**; the graph is not symmetric about the vertical axis.

\Image[paper-1-cubic-exponential.png]
{title=The decreasing cubic exponential, its stationary inflection, and its positive horizontal asymptote}

