= Solution
{parent=a}

Differentiate the <polynomial> and factor:
$$
y'=3x^2-4x-7=(3x-7)(x+1).
$$
The <stationary points> therefore have horizontal coordinates
$$
\boxed{x=-1,\qquad x=7/3.}
$$
For a check, $y''=6x-4$ is negative at the first and positive at the second, giving a local maximum and a <local minimum> respectively.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The second <derivative> is $y''=6x$, so $y''(1)=6>0$. By the <second-derivative test>, the <stationary point> is a **strict <local minimum>**, at $(1,5)$. It is not a global minimum, since this cubic is unbounded below toward negative infinity.

