= Solution
{parent=a}

On $0\leq x\leq1/2$, the <quadratic polynomial> is positive. The required area is therefore its <definite integral> above the horizontal axis:
$$
\mathcal A=\int_0^{1/2}(x^2-3x+2)dx
=\left[\frac{x^3}{3}-\frac{3x^2}{2}+2x\right]_0^{1/2}
=\frac1{24}-\frac38+1=\boxed{\frac23}.
$$
The units are square coordinate units.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Real points require $x\geq0$, and the two branches are
$$
\boxed{y=\pm x^{3/2},\qquad x\geq0.}
$$
This is a <semicubical parabola>, symmetric about the horizontal axis and with a cusp at the origin. The parametrization $x=t^2$, $y=t^3$ traces both branches. Their limiting slopes at the origin are zero, so the common tangent direction is the positive horizontal axis, even though the curve has a cusp there.

\Image[paper-1-semicubical-parabola.png]
{title=The two real branches of the semicubical parabola and their cusp at the origin}

