= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Expanding the <determinant> along the first row gives
$$
\det A=1(4-16)+4(-4+28)+7(16-28)
=-12+96-84=\boxed{0}.
$$
The <matrix trace> is the sum of the diagonal entries, **$\operatorname{tr}A=1+4+1=6$**. Since the <determinant> is the product of the <eigenvalues>, at least one <eigenvalue> is zero. Thus $A$ is singular; the <determinant> alone does not specify the two other <eigenvalues>.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The symmetry between the first and third coordinates makes $(1,0,-1)^{\mathsf T}$ an <eigenvector>, with <eigenvalue> $-6$. In the remaining invariant <plane>, write a <vector> as $(s,t,s)^{\mathsf T}$. Its image is $(8s-4t,-8s+4t,8s-4t)^{\mathsf T}$, so the reduced <eigenvalue> equation has <determinant>
$$
\det\begin{pmatrix}8-\lambda&-4\\-8&4-\lambda\end{pmatrix}
=\lambda(\lambda-12).
$$
The corresponding directions have $t=2s$ for $\lambda=0$ and $t=-s$ for $\lambda=12$. Normalizing gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\lambda=-6&\mathbf e_-=(1,0,-1)^{\mathsf T}/\sqrt2\\[2pt]
\lambda=0&\mathbf e_0=(1,2,1)^{\mathsf T}/\sqrt6\\[2pt]
\lambda=12&\mathbf e_+=(1,-1,1)^{\mathsf T}/\sqrt3.
\end{array}}
$$
Their squared unnormalized norms are $2,6,3$. The pairwise unnormalized <dot products> are $1-1=0$, $1-1=0$, and $1-2+1=0$, so these unit <eigenvectors> are mutually orthogonal. The <eigenvalues> sum to $6$ and have product zero, agreeing with part (a).

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Expand an arbitrary <vector> in the <orthonormal eigenbasis>:
$$
\mathbf r=r_-\mathbf e_-+r_0\mathbf e_0+r_+\mathbf e_+,
\qquad A\mathbf r=-6r_-\mathbf e_-+12r_+\mathbf e_+.
$$
Its image has no component along $\mathbf e_0$, so
$$
\boxed{(A\mathbf r)\cdot\mathbf e_0=0\quad\text{for every }\mathbf r,
\qquad\mathbf e_0=\frac1{\sqrt6}(1,2,1)^{\mathsf T}\ne0.}
$$
Alternatively, symmetry gives $(A\mathbf r)\cdot\mathbf e_0=\mathbf r\cdot A\mathbf e_0=0$. This is <image-kernel orthogonality for an adjoint>, with $A^{\mathsf T}=A$.

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Resolve the input along the three perpendicular eigendirections. The <linear map> **removes the component along $(1,2,1)$, stretches the component along $(1,-1,1)$ by $12$, and reverses and stretches the component along $(1,0,-1)$ by $6$**. Every output lies in the <plane> perpendicular to $(1,2,1)$. A nonzero input in the <kernel of a linear map> $A$ is sent to zero; other nonzero inputs remain in that output <plane>. Equivalently, take the <orthogonal projection> onto this <plane>, then scale by the two signed <eigenvalues> along its perpendicular eigendirections.

