The <complex number> $-1+i$ has <complex modulus> $\sqrt2$ and argument $3\pi/4$. A cube root has modulus $(\sqrt2)^{1/3}=2^{1/6}$, and its argument is one third of $3\pi/4+2k\pi$. Hence the three distinct roots are
$$
\boxed{z_k=2^{1/6}\exp\left[i\left(\frac\pi4+\frac{2k\pi}{3}\right)\right],
\qquad k=0,1,2.}
$$
Their displayed arguments are $\pi/4$, $11\pi/12$, and $19\pi/12$; the last is equivalently $-5\pi/12$ in the principal range.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the exponential definition of the <hyperbolic tangent>:
$$
\frac{e^{2z}-1}{e^{2z}+1}=-i
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
(1+i)e^{2z}=1-i
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad e^{2z}=-i.
$$
The <complex exponential> has modulus one here, so $\operatorname{Re}z=0$. Its arguments satisfy $2\operatorname{Im}z=-\pi/2+2k\pi$. Thus all solutions are
$$
\boxed{z=-\frac{i\pi}{4}+ik\pi,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The denominator is then $1-i\ne0$, so no pole was admitted by cross-multiplication. Substitution also follows from $\tanh(i\theta)=i\tan\theta$ and the period $i\pi$.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Divide the complex <polynomial> by its known factor $z-(2+i)$:
$$
p(z)=(z-2-i)\big[z^2-(2+i)z+(1+i)\big].
$$
The quadratic factors further as $(z-1)(z-1-i)$, because its two proposed roots sum to $2+i$ and multiply to $1+i$. Therefore the remaining solutions are
$$
\boxed{z=1,\qquad z=1+i.}
$$
All three roots are distinct. Complex coefficients do not require nonreal roots to occur in conjugate pairs.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

By <De Moivre's theorem>, $(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)^4=\cos4\theta+i\sin4\theta$. Take real parts of the binomial expansion:
$$
\cos4\theta=\cos^4\theta-6\cos^2\theta\sin^2\theta+\sin^4\theta.
$$
Eliminate $\sin^2\theta$ using $1-\cos^2\theta$ and collect powers:
$$
\boxed{\cos4\theta=8\cos^4\theta-8\cos^2\theta+1.}
$$

