The radial interval is nonempty exactly when $1+\cos\phi\geq3/2$, or $\cos\phi\geq1/2$. Choose the representative angular range $-\pi/3\leq\phi\leq\pi/3$. The <region of integration> is therefore
$$
\boxed{-\frac\pi3\leq\phi\leq\frac\pi3,\qquad
\frac32\leq r\leq1+\cos\phi.}
$$
It is the right-hand cap inside the <cardioid> and outside the <circle> of <radius> $3/2$, symmetric about the horizontal axis. Its boundary intersections have coordinates $(3/4,\pm3\sqrt3/4)$, and the cap reaches $(2,0)$. The radial segment on the horizontal axis runs from $3/2$ to $2$.

\Image[paper-1-cardioid-cap.png]
{title=The integration cap inside a cardioid and outside the concentric circle}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <polar area element> is $r\,dr\,d\phi$. Integrating first in <radius> and using symmetry gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A
&=\frac12\int_{-\pi/3}^{\pi/3}\left[(1+\cos\phi)^2-\frac94\right]d\phi\\
&=\int_0^{\pi/3}\left[-\frac34+2\cos\phi+\frac12\cos2\phi\right]d\phi\\
&=\left[-\frac{3\phi}{4}+2\sin\phi+\frac14\sin2\phi\right]_0^{\pi/3}
=\boxed{\frac{9\sqrt3}{8}-\frac\pi4.}
\end{aligned}
$$
This is the specialization $a=1$, $c=3/2$ of the <polar cap between a cardioid and a circle> area formula.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Substitute $x=r\cos\phi$, $y=r\sin\phi$ and include the <Jacobian determinant> factor $r$. The integrand times the area element becomes
$$
\big(\cos\phi+\sin\phi+r\cos\phi\sin\phi\big)dr\,d\phi.
$$
Both terms containing one factor $\sin\phi$ are odd in $\phi$, while the radial bounds and angular interval are even. They therefore integrate to zero. The remaining <double integral> is
$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{-\pi/3}^{\pi/3}\cos\phi\left(1+\cos\phi-\frac32\right)d\phi\\
&=\int_{-\pi/3}^{\pi/3}\left(\cos^2\phi-\frac12\cos\phi\right)d\phi
=\boxed{\frac\pi3-\frac{\sqrt3}{4}.}
\end{aligned}
$$
The domain stays away from $r=0$, so the denominator of the original integrand causes no singularity in this region.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Translate the variables by $u=x-x_0$, $v=y-y_0$, $w=z-z_0$. The numerator becomes $u+x_0$. The part odd in $u$ integrates to zero, while the constant part factors into three <Gaussian integrals>. Thus
$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=x_0\left(\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-u^2/a^2}du\right)^3\\
&=x_0(a\sqrt\pi)^3
=\boxed{\pi^{3/2}a^3x_0.}
\end{aligned}
$$
The absolute integrability of a <polynomial> times a Gaussian justifies this separation by <Fubini's theorem>. Neither $y_0$ nor $z_0$ affects the result; when $x_0=0$, odd symmetry makes the integral zero.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <gradient> equations factor as
$$
f_x=6(x^2+y^2-25)=0,\qquad f_y=3y(4x-3y)=0.
$$
For $y=0$ they give $x=\pm5$. Otherwise $y=4x/3$, which gives $x=\pm3$ and the matching $y=\pm4$. Thus there are exactly four <stationary points>.

The <Hessian matrix> is
$$
H_f=\begin{pmatrix}12x&12y\\12y&12x-18y\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At $(5,0)$ it is $60I$, and at $(-5,0)$ it is $-60I$. At $(3,4)$ and $(-3,-4)$ its <determinant> is $-3600<0$. The <second-derivative test> therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{point}&\text{type}&f\text{ value}\\\hline
(5,0)&\text{strict local minimum}&-500\\
(-5,0)&\text{strict local maximum}&500\\
(3,4)&\text{saddle point}&-300\\
(-3,-4)&\text{saddle point}&300.
\end{array}}
$$
The extrema are local: along $y=0$ the cubic is unbounded both above and below.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <partial derivatives> give $x^3=9y$ and $y^3=9x$. If either coordinate is zero, both vanish. Otherwise substitution gives $x^8=3^8$, so the real <stationary points> are $(0,0)$, $(3,3)$ and $(-3,-3)$.

The <Hessian matrix> is
$$
H_g=\begin{pmatrix}12x^2&-36\\-36&12y^2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At the origin its <eigenvalues> are $36$ and $-36$, so it is a saddle at level zero. At either of the other points its <eigenvalues> are $72$ and $144$, so both are strict minima at level $-162$. In fact,
$$
g=(x^2-y^2)^2+2(xy-9)^2-162\geq-162,
$$
with equality only at those two points. Hence
$$
\boxed{(0,0):\text{ saddle, }g=0;\qquad
(3,3),(-3,-3):\text{ global minima, }g=-162.}
$$
For levels $-162<c<0$, two separate closed contours surround the two minima. At $c=0$ the contours form two lobes, in the first and third quadrants, meeting at the origin. Their limiting tangent directions there are the coordinate axes because the leading term is $-36xy$. On the diagonal the outer zero-level points are $(\pm3\sqrt2,\pm3\sqrt2)$. Positive levels form one closed outer contour enclosing both wells; there are no contours below $-162$. The graph is symmetric under exchanging $x,y$ and under reversing both signs, but not under a quarter-turn. These features are the $a=3$ case of the <quartic double well with bilinear coupling>.

\Image[paper-1-quartic-contours.png]
{title=The quartic function's two minima, saddle, and transition from two contour loops to one}

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With the usual real normalization, the <Fourier series> is
$$
f(x)\sim\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\big[a_n\cos nx+b_n\sin nx\big],
$$
with <Fourier coefficients>
$$
\boxed{a_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)\cos nx\,dx\quad(n\geq0),\qquad
b_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)\sin nx\,dx\quad(n\geq1).}
$$
These formulas follow from <Fourier orthogonality>. Integrability is needed to define the coefficients, and periodicity alone does not guarantee pointwise equality with the series. For a piecewise smooth periodic <function>, the <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem> gives the average of the one-sided limits at a jump and the <function> value at a <continuity> point.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The periodic continuation is $g_{\rm per}(x)=e^{x-2k\pi}$ on each interval $-\pi+2k\pi\leq x<\pi+2k\pi$. Thus the plot repeats an increasing exponential segment on each of the three requested periods, jumping down from $e^\pi$ to $e^{-\pi}$ at odd multiples of $\pi$.

At $x=\pi$, the left and right limits are $e^\pi$ and $e^{-\pi}$. The <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem> therefore makes the <Fourier series> converge to
$$
\boxed{\frac{e^\pi+e^{-\pi}}2=\cosh\pi.}
$$
The actual half-open periodic definition has $g_{\rm per}(\pi)=e^{-\pi}$; the Fourier value at the jump need not equal that assigned endpoint value.

\Image[paper-1-periodic-exponential.png]
{title=Three periods of the exponential continuation and the Fourier-series midpoint at a jump}

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Take the odd extension $H(x)=x(\pi-|x|)$ on $[-\pi,\pi]$ and then extend with period $2\pi$. It agrees with the required arch for $0\leq x\leq\pi$ and is continuous at its joins. Its <Fourier sine series> has
$$
b_n=\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi x(\pi-x)\sin nx\,dx.
$$
For clarity, let $h=x(\pi-x)$, so $h'=\pi-2x$ and $h''=-2$. Two integrations by parts, with the first endpoint term zero, give
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_0^\pi h\sin nx\,dx
&=\frac1n\int_0^\pi h'\cos nx\,dx\\
&=-\frac1{n^2}\int_0^\pi h''\sin nx\,dx
=\frac{2[1-(-1)^n]}{n^3}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Consequently $b_n=4[1-(-1)^n]/(\pi n^3)$: the even coefficients vanish and the odd ones are $8/(\pi n^3)$. This proves
$$
\boxed{h(x)=\frac8\pi\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{\sin((2n+1)x)}{(2n+1)^3},\qquad0\leq x\leq\pi.}
$$
The <Fourier sine series of an odd quadratic arch> converges to this continuous extension; its coefficients are also absolutely summable. Finally evaluate at $x=\pi/2$. The left side is $\pi^2/4$ and the <sine> factors are $(-1)^n$, so
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n}{(2n+1)^3}=\frac{\pi^3}{32}.}
$$
The evaluation is at a <continuity> point, so it does not invoke a jump midpoint.

= 18
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $p_j=\mathbb P(X=j)$, with $\sum_{j=0}^Np_j=1$. The <expected value>, <variance>, and strict lower-tail <probability> are
$$
\boxed{\mu=\sum_{j=0}^Njp_j,\qquad
\sigma^2=\sum_{j=0}^N(j-\mu)^2p_j
=\sum_{j=0}^Nj^2p_j-\mu^2,\qquad
\mathbb P(X<Y)=\sum_{j=0}^{Y-1}p_j.}
$$
The upper summation limit $Y-1$ is essential because the inequality is strict and the variable is integer-valued.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Count unordered sets of distinct labelled fish: there are $\binom NM$ possible hauls. To obtain exactly $j$ trout, choose $j$ labels from the $K$ trout and $M-j$ from the $N-K$ carp, giving $\binom Kj\binom{N-K}{M-j}$ possibilities. Uniform <sampling without replacement> therefore gives the <hypergeometric distribution>
$$
\boxed{p_j=\frac{\binom Kj\binom{N-K}{M-j}}{\binom NM},
\quad\max(0,M-N+K)\leq j\leq\min(M,K).}
$$
Outside this integer range the <probability> is zero. If ordered catches were counted instead, every haul would have $M!$ orders and that factor would cancel from the <probability> ratio.

Here equal catchability means equal selection <probability> for each individual remaining fish. It does not <mean> that the two species have <probability> $1/2$ on each draw regardless of their populations. This interpretation is the one that yields the stated hypergeometric law.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The total number of two-fish hauls is $\binom{10}{2}=45$. Selecting both from the eight carp gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X=0)=\frac{\binom20\binom82}{45}=\frac{28}{45}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose one of the two trout and one of the eight carp. The <hypergeometric distribution> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X=1)=\frac{\binom21\binom81}{45}=\frac{16}{45}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Only one labelled haul contains both trout, giving
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X=2)=\frac{\binom22\binom80}{45}=\frac1{45}.}
$$

= Solution
{parent=c}

The three preceding <probabilities> satisfy
$$
\boxed{\frac{28}{45}+\frac{16}{45}+\frac1{45}=1.}
$$
Their <expected value> and second <moment> are
$$
\mu=\frac{16+2}{45}=\frac25,\qquad
\mathbb E[X^2]=\frac{16+4}{45}=\frac49.
$$
Subtracting the squared <mean> gives
$$
\boxed{\mu=\frac25,\qquad\sigma^2=\frac49-\frac4{25}=\frac{64}{225}.}
$$
The value $\mu=2(2/10)$ also follows from <linearity of expectation>. The <variance> includes the finite-population reduction caused by <sampling without replacement>.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

When only a tiny fraction of either species is removed, the trout fraction among the remaining fish stays nearly constant at $p=K/N$. Successive catches are then approximately independent trials with that fixed success <probability>. The count therefore approaches the <binomial distribution>
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X=j)\approx\binom Mj p^j(1-p)^{M-j},\qquad p=K/N.}
$$
The approximation comes from negligible depletion, not from an exact <independence> property of <sampling without replacement>.

= 19
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write the formal <Maclaurin series> as $\sum_{n\geq0}a_nx^n$, where $a_n=f^{(n)}(0)/n!$. If the successive nonzero coefficient ratios have a limit, the <ratio test> gives
$$
\left|\frac{a_{n+1}x^{n+1}}{a_nx^n}\right|
=|x|\left|\frac{f^{(n+1)}(0)}{(n+1)f^{(n)}(0)}\right|\longrightarrow\frac{|x|}{R},
$$
so the series converges absolutely for $|x|<R$, where
$$
\boxed{R=\lim_{n\to\infty}\left|\frac{(n+1)f^{(n)}(0)}{f^{(n+1)}(0)}\right|.}
$$
If that ratio does not exist, the <Cauchy-Hadamard theorem> gives the general formula $R^{-1}=\limsup|a_n|^{1/n}$. The <radius of a Maclaurin series from successive derivatives> concerns convergence of the series; equality to the original <function> additionally requires <analyticity> or a vanishing <Taylor remainder>. Mere differentiability, or even smoothness, does not ensure that equality.

For the specified logarithm,
$$
\ln(2+x)=\ln2+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}x^n}{n2^n},\qquad |x|<2.
$$
The absolute successive-term ratio tends to $|x|/2$, so **the interval of <absolute convergence> is $(-2,2)$**. At $x=2$ the nonconstant terms are the alternating harmonic series, which converges conditionally to $\ln2$, giving the full sum $\ln4$. At $x=-2$ all nonconstant terms are $-1/n$, so the series diverges to $-\infty$; the original logarithm is also undefined there. Thus the full real convergence interval is **$(-2,2]$**.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Multiply the <Maclaurin series> $e^{-x}=1-x+x^2/2+O(x^3)$ by the <binomial series> $(1+x)^{-1/2}=1-x/2+3x^2/8+O(x^3)$. The constant, linear and quadratic coefficients are therefore
$$
\boxed{e^{-x}(1+x)^{-1/2}=1-\frac32x+\frac{11}{8}x^2+O(x^3).}
$$

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

There is an indexing defect in the original PDF: the term at $n=1$ is undefined because $\ln1=0$. Thus the series as literally indexed is not defined. The natural finite-index repair is to start at $n=2$, and that repaired series still **diverges**.

Indeed, for $n\geq2$ the terms are positive and
$$
\frac{u_{n+1}}{u_n}
=2\frac{n\ln n}{(n+1)\ln(n+1)}\longrightarrow2>1.
$$
They eventually increase at a geometric rate and do not tend to zero. Since the <terms of a convergent series tend to zero>, no choice of a finite replacement for the missing first term could make the tail converge.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the bound $|\sin n|\leq1$:
$$
\left|\frac{\sin n}{n^2}\right|\leq\frac1{n^2}.
$$
The comparison <p-series> has exponent $2>1$ and converges. Hence, by the <comparison test for series>, the original series is **absolutely convergent**. No alternating-sign or monotonicity assumption about $\sin n$ is needed.

= d
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Index the terms from $n=0$:
$$
S(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n+4}}{(n+3)n!}.
$$
Its absolute successive-term ratio tends to zero for every fixed $x$, so it converges everywhere. Divide by $x$ temporarily and use the <exponential series divided by a shifted index> with $k=3$:
$$
\frac{S(x)}x=\int_0^x t^2e^t\,dt
=\big[e^t(t^2-2t+2)\big]_0^x.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{S(x)=x\big[(x^2-2x+2)e^x-2\big].}
$$
At $x=0$ both the original series and this formula are zero, so the temporary division loses no value. The termwise <integration> is justified by uniform convergence of the exponential series on the finite <integration> interval.

= 20
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=20}

At a regular constrained <stationary point>, the <Lagrange multiplier> equations are $f_x=\lambda g_x$, $f_y=\lambda g_y$. Eliminating the multiplier gives
$$
f_xg_y-f_yg_x=\lambda g_xg_y-\lambda g_yg_x=0.
$$
Thus the required <Lagrange multiplier determinant condition in two variables> is
$$
\boxed{\det\begin{pmatrix}f_x&g_x\\f_y&g_y\end{pmatrix}_{(a,b)}=0.}
$$
If the constraint <gradient> vanishes, this <determinant> is zero automatically, although the regular multiplier theorem cannot be applied. The condition is necessary and is not sufficient to classify the constrained point. This addresses the original unnumbered task before part (a).

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Maximizing the squared distance avoids differentiating a square root. The <Lagrange multiplier> equations for $f=x^2+y^2$ are
$$
2x=\lambda(2x+y),\qquad2y=\lambda(x+2y),\qquad x^2+y^2+xy=4.
$$
Adding and subtracting the first two gives $(2-3\lambda)(x+y)=0$ and $(2-\lambda)(x-y)=0$. The feasible <stationary points> therefore lie on $x=y$ or $x=-y$. The first case gives $(x,y)=(\pm2/\sqrt3,\pm2/\sqrt3)$ and squared distance $8/3$. The second gives $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2)$, with squared distance $8$.

The <ellipse> is compact and its constraint <gradient> does not vanish on the curve, so an extremum exists among these points. A direct global check is $4=f+xy\geq f/2$, using $xy\geq-f/2$. Equality occurs when $x=-y$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{d_{\max}=2\sqrt2,\qquad\text{at }(2,-2),\ (-2,2).}
$$

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Assume positive corridor widths $a,b$. Write the line through the two wall-contact endpoints in intercept form. Requiring it to pass through the inner corner gives
$$
\frac a{a+X}+\frac b{b+Y}=1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad XY=ab,
\qquad X,Y>0.
$$
Its squared length is $F=(a+X)^2+(b+Y)^2$. Apply <Lagrange multipliers> to this <function> subject to $G=XY-ab=0$:
$$
2(a+X)=\lambda Y,\qquad2(b+Y)=\lambda X.
$$
Multiplying by $X$ and $Y$ respectively eliminates the multiplier, giving $X(a+X)=Y(b+Y)$. Substitute $Y=ab/X$ and multiply by $X^2$:
$$
X^3(a+X)=ab^2(a+X),\qquad
\boxed{(X^3-ab^2)(X+a)=0.}
$$
The branch $X=-a$ is geometrically inadmissible. Thus $X=a^{1/3}b^{2/3}$ and $Y=a^{2/3}b^{1/3}$. If $A=a^{1/3}$ and $B=b^{1/3}$, the two intercepts become $A(A^2+B^2)$ and $B(A^2+B^2)$, so
$$
F_{\min}=(A^2+B^2)^3,
\qquad\boxed{L=(a^{2/3}+b^{2/3})^{3/2}.}
$$
Along $XY=ab$ in the positive quadrant, $F\to\infty$ as $X\to0^+$ or $X\to\infty$, and this is its only <stationary point>. It is therefore the minimum of the longest sticks that fit at each orientation. A stick being turned must pass through every intermediate orientation, so this narrowest clearance is precisely the maximum carryable length, rather than a maximum of $F$. This is the <ladder around a right-angled corner> problem, with a zero-width stick and wall contact allowed as in the ideal geometry.

\Image[paper-1-ladder-corner.png]
{title=The critical ladder position touching the two outer walls and the inner corridor corner}
